/**
 * @defgroup mover_efficiency_calculator Mover Efficiency Calculator
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Calculates the fraction of pump or fan shaft input delivered to the fluid at a supplied duty.
 * @details
 * How much of the power supplied to a pump or fan shaft reaches the fluid? Comparing useful fluid
 * output with shaft input helps evaluate an operating point and distinguish mover losses from motor
 * and drive losses. This calculator evaluates that ratio from a supplied flow, head or pressure duty,
 * and shaft power. It does not estimate shaft power from electrical measurements.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result and Interpretation}
 * Both paths return a dimensionless efficiency fraction. Fluid power is first converted from kW
 * to hp so that numerator and denominator share the same units:
 * @formula{mover-efficiency-ratio; \eta_m=\frac{P_f/c_{hp}}{P_s}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_m; Mover efficiency as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_f; Power delivered to the fluid at the supplied duty; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{c_{hp}; Retained horsepower conversion, 0.746; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{P_s; Power supplied at the mover shaft; \horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A result of 0.75 means that 75% of the shaft input reaches the fluid under the supplied duty and
 * pressure convention. Multiply the fraction by 100 only when displaying a percentage. A result
 * greater than one or less than zero is returned unchanged; it calls for checking units, measurements,
 * signs and measurement conventions before interpreting equipment performance. For ordinary consuming
 * equipment with consistent positive inputs, useful output should not exceed shaft input.
 *
 * Shaft input is measured at the pump or fan shaft, after any intervening drive. Do not substitute
 * motor electrical kW or motor shaft hp upstream of a lossy drive. This ratio excludes upstream motor
 * and drive losses and therefore does not represent overall electrical-to-fluid efficiency.
 *
 * @heading{Select the Duty and Establish Consistent Inputs}
 * Use the pump path for liquid flow in US gpm, pump head in feet of the pumped fluid, dimensionless
 * specific gravity, and pump shaft input in hp. Head is the supplied energy-per-weight rise across
 * the pump; do not substitute pressure in psi. This path uses flow and head, not the separate
 * positive-displacement pressure-based calculation used by pump assessments.
 *
 * Use the fan path for gas flow in cfm, inlet and outlet gauge pressures in inches of water column,
 * a supplied dimensionless compressibility correction, velocity pressure in inches of water column,
 * and fan shaft input in hp. Pressures must use a common reference and compatible measurement planes.
 * All fields are required, including velocity pressure; explicitly supply zero when no velocity-pressure
 * term is to be subtracted. The correction is supplied by the caller and is not a ratio of specific heats.
 *
 * Use duty and shaft measurements for the same operating point. The calculator does not reconcile
 * different measurement times, correct instrumentation, convert user-selected units, or identify a
 * suitable pressure convention.
 *
 * @heading{Why Flow and Duty Determine the Useful Output}
 * Liquid flow carries weight through a head rise. The pump path obtains fluid power from the
 * @ref fluid_power_calculator "Fluid Power Calculator":
 * @formula{mover-efficiency-pump-fluid-power; P_{f,p}=\frac{c_{hp}Q_pH\,SG}{C_p}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_{f,p}; Power delivered to the liquid; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{c_{hp}; Retained horsepower conversion, 0.746; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{Q_p; Liquid volumetric flow in US gallons per minute; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{H; Pump head in feet of the pumped fluid; \foot}
 * @symrow{SG; Fluid specific gravity; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_p; Retained flow-head horsepower divisor, 3961.38; \gallon\foot\per\minute\per\horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Gas flow through a pressure rise transfers pressure-volume work. The fan path obtains fluid power
 * using the supplied compressibility correction and the retained pressure convention:
 * @formula{mover-efficiency-fan-fluid-power; P_{f,f}=\frac{c_{hp}Q_f(p_o-p_i-p_v)K}{C_f}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_{f,f}; Power delivered to the gas; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{c_{hp}; Retained horsepower conversion, 0.746; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{Q_f; Gas volumetric flow; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{p_o,p_i; Outlet and inlet gauge pressures with a common reference; \inchWaterColumn}
 * @symrow{p_v; Supplied velocity pressure subtracted from the rise; \inchWaterColumn}
 * @symrow{K; Supplied fan compressibility correction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_f; Retained flow-pressure horsepower divisor, 6362; \cubicFoot\inchWaterColumn\per\minute\per\horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The effective fan rise is outlet minus inlet minus velocity pressure. This is the retained Suite
 * convention. The method does not determine whether supplied pressures are static or total, reconstruct
 * velocity pressure, or assign a standardized static/total efficiency classification. Establish a
 * compatible measurement convention upstream, and avoid subtracting velocity pressure again from an
 * already adjusted rise. Negative inlet gauge pressure can contribute to a positive effective rise.
 *
 * Dividing fluid power by shaft input expresses the useful output per unit input. At a fixed duty,
 * increasing shaft input reduces the calculated ratio. A real equipment change may also change the
 * operating point; this calculation does not predict that coupled response.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Pump Example}
 * For specific gravity 1.5, flow 1000 US gpm, head 125 ft, and shaft input 125 hp, the fluid-power
 * calculation gives approximately 35.31 kW, or 47.33 hp. Applying the ratio:
 * @formula{mover-efficiency-pump-example;
 *   \eta_{m,p}=\frac{\left(0.746\times1000\times125\times1.5/3961.38\right)/0.746}{125}
 *             \approx0.3787
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{m,p}; Calculated pump efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.746; Retained horsepower conversion; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{1000; Supplied liquid flow; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{125\text{ in numerator}; Supplied pump head; \foot}
 * @symrow{1.5; Supplied specific gravity; \unitless}
 * @symrow{3961.38; Retained pump divisor; \gallon\foot\per\minute\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{125\text{ in denominator}; Supplied pump shaft input; \horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * About 37.87% of the supplied pump shaft input reaches the liquid at this operating point. Motor
 * electrical input would also need to account for motor and drive efficiency; it cannot be recovered
 * from this mover efficiency alone.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Fan Example}
 * For flow 2000 cfm, shaft input 5 hp, inlet gauge pressure 20 inches water column, outlet gauge
 * pressure 25 inches water column, compressibility correction 1.4, and velocity pressure 1 inch water
 * column, the effective rise is 4 inches water column. Fluid power is approximately 1.313 kW,
 * or 1.760 hp:
 * @formula{mover-efficiency-fan-example;
 *   \eta_{m,f}=\frac{\left(0.746\times2000\times(25-20-1)\times1.4/6362\right)/0.746}{5}
 *             \approx0.3521
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{m,f}; Calculated fan efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.746; Retained horsepower conversion; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{2000; Supplied gas flow; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{25,20; Supplied outlet and inlet gauge pressures; \inchWaterColumn}
 * @symrow{1; Supplied velocity pressure; \inchWaterColumn}
 * @symrow{1.4; Supplied compressibility correction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{6362; Retained fan divisor; \cubicFoot\inchWaterColumn\per\minute\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{5; Supplied fan shaft input; \horsepower}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The result represents about 35.21% of the shaft input delivered to the gas under this pressure
 * convention. With the other inputs unchanged, omitting the 1-inch velocity-pressure term would
 * give about 44.01%. Pressure measurement conventions therefore directly affect the interpreted ratio.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions, Boundaries and Inappropriate Uses}
 * Normal consuming-equipment duties use positive shaft power, positive flow and specific gravity,
 * positive pump head or effective fan rise, and an appropriate supplied fan correction. These are
 * interpretation guidelines, not enforced ranges. The calculator evaluates supplied values directly
 * and does not clamp results to zero through one or multiply them by 100.
 *
 * With finite arithmetic and no intermediate overflow, zero fluid power divided by nonzero shaft
 * power produces zero efficiency. Nonzero fluid power divided by zero shaft power produces signed
 * infinity; zero divided by zero produces NaN. Negative shaft input or fluid output affects the
 * result's sign. Signed zero, infinity, NaN, underflow and overflow follow floating-point arithmetic,
 * including the intermediate kW-to-hp conversion. Infinite flow multiplied by zero head or zero
 * effective fan rise produces NaN, as does infinite fluid power divided by infinite shaft input.
 * These arithmetic results are not automatically interpreted as valid performance or recoverable power.
 *
 * This method does not select equipment, predict pump/fan curves, assess cavitation or transients,
 * estimate annual energy or cost, derive compressibility corrections, or model a general compressor.
 * It does not provide a best-efficiency-point prediction or an independent efficiency guarantee.
 *
 * @heading{Constants and Provenance}
 * The result is an efficiency fraction. The @ref fluid_power_calculator "Fluid Power Calculator"
 * supplies the 3961.38 and 6362 divisors, 0.746 conversion and velocity-pressure subtraction.
 * Their exact historical divisor provenance is unverified;
 * the physical explanation above does not establish the final digits of those constants.
 *
 * @heading{Calculation Interface}
 * C++ callers include motorDriven/pumpFan/mover_efficiency.h and use
 * mover_efficiency::calculatePump(mover_efficiency::PumpInput) or
 * mover_efficiency::calculateFan(mover_efficiency::FanInput).
 * WASM/TypeScript callers use calculatePumpMoverEfficiency(PumpMoverEfficiencyInput) or
 * calculateFanMoverEfficiency(FanMoverEfficiencyInput). Inputs are required plain objects with
 * camelCase fields, including moverShaftPower in both paths; results are numeric fractions.
 * Neither the inputs nor results require delete().
 */
